// The FFI boundary, and the one place in elp where duplication semantics
// cannot be inferred.
//
// `aliasing.md` closes on an assumption: mutable aliasing is obtainable only
// from `Confined<T>`, `Synchronized<T>`, or a closure that captured one, so
// field-wise conformance always sees it and nothing needs declaring. That
// holds because elp has no user-constructible interior mutability — the
// unsafe surface is `@external` and nothing else.
//
// This file is that boundary. A `sqlite3*` is a pointer: copying it aliases
// a database connection, and no amount of looking at elp source can tell.
// So this is the single surface where a declaration has to state what the
// compiler would otherwise work out, and this file exists to put the cases
// on the page. Not all of the spellings below are decided.
//
// `@external(.OBJC)` already exists in the corpus
// (`BlogStyle/internal/api.elp`) and the parser already accepts body-less
// declarations for it. What it never carried is *which library* a symbol
// comes from, which is what `library =` adds here against the `links`
// allow-list in `Package.elp`.
//
// An earlier draft of this file invented an `extern` keyword. That was
// wrong twice over: `@external` already does the job, and `reactivity.md`
// rejects new lowercase intrinsic keywords as a matter of policy — it is why
// `&mut` is not a spelling either.
//
// The block form below is the reason that keyword is not missed, and it is
// not an FFI feature. A macro is not an entry in the program at all — it
// receives the AST of what follows it and generates the declarations the
// program actually contains (`macros.md`). A declaration list is an AST node
// like any other, so `library = "sqlite3"` is written once because the
// attribute is written once.
//
// Which is also why the braces introduce no scope and no namespace:
// `sqlite3_open` is `sqlite3_open`, because these braces are never in the
// program. They are the generator's input. What reaches this module is the
// macro's output — ordinary module-level declarations.

// GAP 1, RESOLVED — an opaque handle's duplication semantics are an
// ordinary conformance, declared rather than derived.
//
// The draft of this file asked three questions and proposed a strawman that
// put all three in `@external`'s arguments:
//
//   @external(.C, library = "sqlite3", duplication = .aliasing, threads = .confined)
//
// Two of the three needed no new syntax at all, and the strawman was
// inventing `.aliasing`/`.independent`/`.illegal` for exactly what
// `Move`/`Copy`/`Duplicate` already mean. The answers:
//
//   - duplication -> the conformance, written below.
//   - may it cross a thread? -> `Sendable`, declared not derived. Still
//     blocked in fact by GAP 2: SQLite's answer is a runtime value.
//   - who releases it, exactly once? -> still open. See `db.elp`.
//
// The draft also mis-diagnosed *why* the compiler could not help. It said an
// external type "has no fields for field-wise `Copy` to be wise about".
// Field-wise was not failing to see the handle — `db.elp` held it as
// `&Sqlite3`, `memory-model.md` Finding 6 makes `&T` unconditionally `Copy`,
// so the rule looked, applied, and said *yes*. `Connection implements Move`
// was the author declining a `Copy` the compiler would have granted, with
// nothing stopping the next person granting it.
//
// `aliasing.md` closes it with three parts: a field may not hold a borrow, a
// foreign handle is a value rather than a borrow, and an `@external type`
// must declare its tier because silence derives the dangerous answer.
@external(.C, library = "sqlite3") {
	export type Sqlite3
	export type Sqlite3Stmt
}

// `Move` because *this* program releases them: `sqlite3_close` and
// `sqlite3_finalize` must each run exactly once, and `Move` is the only tier
// where "the source is dead" makes that checkable.
//
// Worth saying what this is NOT, because generalising from SQLite alone
// would be wrong. GLFW and Vulkan hand back pointers they own and never
// expect back — `VkQueue`, `VkPhysicalDevice`, `GLFWmonitor*` — and the
// right tier for those is `Copy`: you never destroy one, duplicating is
// free, and two copies have nothing to observe about each other. Same rule,
// opposite answer, which is why the declaration is mandatory rather than
// defaulted.
Sqlite3     implements Move
Sqlite3Stmt implements Move

// One block for the whole library's functions. Note what stayed on each
// line: `export`. Nothing distributes it, and nothing distributes anything —
// the macro was handed declarations that said `export` and emits
// declarations that say `export`. So a block of mixed visibility is writable
// for free, which this file would need the moment one symbol went internal.
@external(.C, library = "sqlite3") {
	// GAP 2 — SQLite's thread discipline is a **runtime** value, and
	// `Confined`/`Synchronized` is a **static** distinction.
	//
	// `sqlite3_threadsafe()` returns which of three modes the library was
	// compiled with: single-thread, multi-thread (one connection per
	// thread), or serialized (connections shared freely). The answer is not
	// knowable when elp compiles.
	//
	// This is the sharpest thing in this file, because `aliasing.md` assumes
	// the discipline is a property of the type. Here it is a property of a
	// build of a library discovered at startup. The honest options:
	//
	//   - declare the strictest (`.confined`) and lose the serialized mode's
	//     capability for everyone;
	//   - expose both types and make the caller pick, with a runtime check
	//     that panics on mismatch;
	//   - make the discipline a type parameter resolved at link time, which
	//     no part of this language does today.
	//
	// Nothing in `aliasing.md`, `reactivity.md` or `dispatch.md` covers a
	// capability that is real but not statically known. That is new — and
	// `name-resolution.md`'s `minimumVersion` gap is the same shape, which
	// makes it a pattern rather than a one-off.
	export fn sqlite3_threadsafe() -> int

	// GAP 3, RESOLVED — a scalar out-parameter is `&Optional<T>`.
	//
	// The C signature is:
	//
	//   int sqlite3_open(const char *filename, sqlite3 **ppDb)
	//
	// An earlier draft of this file wrote the second parameter as
	// `out &OpaqueHandle` and the call site as `&out` — an undeclared
	// binding, which was placeholder shorthand that should never have been
	// written. There is no `out` in this language.
	//
	// The difficulty was never mutability direction, so `&mut` would have
	// described the wrong axis: you cannot take a mutable reference to a
	// `Sqlite3` that does not exist yet. It is **emptiness**, and
	// `Optional<T>` is already a slot that may be empty.
	// `Optional<Sqlite3>` niche-optimises to a nullable pointer, so
	// `&db` *is* `sqlite3**` at the ABI.
	//
	// And `memory-model.md` Finding 4 now says what makes the write legal:
	// at an `@external` declaration `&T` is read-write, because Finding 4
	// infers mutability from the callee's body and here the callee is C.
	//
	// What stays irreducible: the status code and the slot are two returns
	// the C ABI does not relate, so `0` is the only evidence the slot was
	// written. `db.elp` reads an `Optional` and lets the code say whether to
	// trust it.
	export fn sqlite3_open(path &CString, db &Optional<Sqlite3>) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_close(db Sqlite3) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_errmsg(db &Sqlite3) -> &CString

	export fn sqlite3_prepare_v2(
		db &Sqlite3,
		sql &CString,
		bytes int,
		stmt &Optional<Sqlite3Stmt>,
		tail &Optional<CString>,
	) -> int

	export fn sqlite3_step(stmt &Sqlite3Stmt) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_finalize(stmt Sqlite3Stmt) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_bind_text(stmt &Sqlite3Stmt, index int, value &CString, bytes int, free &OpaqueHandle) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_bind_int(stmt &Sqlite3Stmt, index int, value int) -> int
	export fn sqlite3_column_int(stmt &Sqlite3Stmt, column int) -> int

	// GAP 5 — a returned pointer whose lifetime is governed by an unrelated
	// call.
	//
	// `sqlite3_column_text` returns a pointer into SQLite's own buffer that
	// is invalidated by the next `sqlite3_step`, `sqlite3_reset` or
	// `sqlite3_finalize` on the same statement. Nothing in the *type* says
	// so.
	//
	// elp's borrow story (`memory-model.md` Finding 4) infers `&T`/`&mut T`
	// from callee usage, which cannot express "this borrow dies when some
	// other method is called on a different object". Rust cannot express it
	// either without a hand-written lifetime, which is why every real
	// binding copies the bytes out immediately. So the rule is probably "a
	// borrow out of an `@external` fn must be consumed before the next
	// external call on a related handle", and nobody has written that down.
	export fn sqlite3_column_text(stmt &Sqlite3Stmt, column int) -> &CString
}

// GAP 4, RESOLVED — `library = "sqlite3"` was written fourteen times, and
// the fix was not the one this file proposed.
//
// The draft offered "`library =` is required only when the package links
// more than one", and rejected it as the kind of conditional requirement
// this language refuses everywhere else. It then rejected grouping too, on
// the grounds that a grouping form is a new declaration shape, which is what
// `@external` being an attribute was supposed to avoid.
//
// Both objections were about the wrong thing. The grouping form is not a
// form of `@external` — it is what a macro's input is allowed to be, and a
// macro is a code generator rather than a participant in the program. So
// nothing about FFI is involved and `@CodeGen(Copy) { ... }` over a run of
// records gets it for the same reason.
//
// Worth recording: because the macro receives the *group*, it can generate
// code that spans the group — three types plus an enum over them plus one
// dispatch table — which N separate expansions cannot. An intermediate
// draft made the block a desugaring into N copies of the attribute, which
// was safe and strictly weaker, and that is the capability it gave up.
//
// The lesson generalises past this file. The repetition read as an argument
// for weakening the attribute's own rules, and it was really a question
// about what a macro may be handed.

// A second block, because the arguments differ. Whether a nested
// `@external` inside the block above would be an error or an override is
// `@external`'s own business rather than a rule of the language — it
// receives the AST and it decides. Written as two blocks either way, so a
// reader never computes a declaration's real arguments from two places.
//
// GAP 6 — `CString` and `OpaqueHandle` are invented here and belong in the
// stdlib.
//
// `strings.md` decides that `string` is a sequence of Unicode scalar values
// with guaranteed UTF-8. A `const char *` is neither: it is bytes with a
// terminator and no encoding guarantee, and SQLite will hand back whatever
// was stored. So conversion is fallible in one direction and allocating in
// the other, and `strings.md`'s open "byte access" question is on the
// critical path for any FFI at all.
//
// `OpaqueHandle` is standing in for `void*` / `T**`, which is worse: it is
// the thing the type system exists to prevent, needed in exactly one place.
//
// GAP 8's resolution gives this a second, harder reason to be stdlib's —
// see below.
@external(.C) {
	export type CString
	export type OpaqueHandle
}

// Both have to declare a tier now, and both are awkward in a way that is
// worth recording rather than smoothing over.
//
// `CString` is `Move` because the elp side allocates it (`CString.from(path)`)
// and something has to free it — so it is the owned-handle row of
// `aliasing.md`'s table, with the same at-least-once hole as a connection.
// Note that this is *not* the `const char *` SQLite hands back: that is a
// transient view, the third row, and no tier fits it. The binding copies it
// out immediately (`Statement.textAt` returns `Optional<string>`) and never
// lets the pointer escape `internal/`.
//
// `OpaqueHandle` is the one that does not belong in this scheme at all. It
// stands in for `void*` and `T**`, so "duplicating it" has no meaning
// independent of what it currently points at. `Move` is the conservative
// answer and the real answer is GAP 3: out-parameters should not be modelled
// as a type.
CString      implements Move
OpaqueHandle implements Move

// GAP 7 — a C callback cannot be an elp closure.
//
// `sqlite3_exec` takes `int (*callback)(void*, int, char**, char**)`: a bare
// function pointer plus a `void*` for state. elp closures capture
// (`functions.md`), and a capturing closure has no C representation — which
// is why the `void*` exists in the C API in the first place.
//
// `functions.md` says the function-type spelling is `fn(T1, T2) -> R` with
// no parameter names and says nothing about whether a non-capturing closure
// is a distinct type. It would have to be, for this to be declarable at all.
//
// Left undeclared rather than guessed. `error-handling.md` already notes
// that a panic crossing an `extern "C"` boundary aborts the process, so an
// elp callback invoked from C is a boundary in both directions.

// GAP 8, RESOLVED — two packages could declare the same symbol differently,
// and the orphan rule has no analogue because there is no contract on the
// far side of a shared library.
//
// The problem: any other elp package could write its own
// `@external(.C, library = "sqlite3") fn sqlite3_open(...)` with a
// *different* signature, each locally correct. One program has one C symbol
// namespace, C has no namespacing, and the linker resolves by name — so both
// get the same address and one of them is wrong.
//
// Why the orphan rule cannot reach it: it works because elp owns both
// operands. Some package defines `T`, some package defines `I`, and "only
// those two may declare the conformance" names a real party either way.
// Nobody defines `sqlite3_open`. It is a name in a symbol table with no
// types attached and no author to appeal to.
//
// `name-resolution.md` now imposes the uniqueness one level up instead: the
// symbol has no owner, but **a library has exactly one claimant** — a
// `links` entry is exclusive across a program, like a package version. That
// is the orphan rule's shape moved from the type to the package, and Cargo
// reached the same rule under the same key name. What exclusivity leaves
// (one package disagreeing with itself, two libraries exporting one name) is
// caught by a whole-program agreement check, which elp can afford because
// resolution is already global.
//
// A NEW FINDING falls out of that check, and it is why GAP 6 is now
// load-bearing rather than tidy-up. Comparing two declarations of
// `sqlite3_open` cannot compare *elp* types: this file declares its own
// `CString` and `OpaqueHandle`, and another package would declare its own
// with its own names. Two ABI-identical signatures would read as a
// disagreement, and two genuinely different ones could read as a match.
//
// So the agreement check compares ABI, not elp type identity — or `CString`
// and `OpaqueHandle` are stdlib's, there is one of each in the program, and
// the comparison is ordinary type equality. The second is obviously better
// and it means GAP 6 has to be closed before GAP 8's check can be
// implemented at all.

// vim: ft=kotlin
